":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/23","text":"Number of keywords"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Number of keywords","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":559.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":350.6666666666667,"r":418.0,"t":569.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,18],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/24","text":"Number of keywords"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 3. Automatically annotating sub-cellular localization for proteomes five","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":467.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":38.0,"r":288.6666666666667,"t":480.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,78],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/25","text":"Table 3. Automatically annotating sub-cellular localization for proteomes five"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"@Nprot: Number of proteins in proteome; Number of proteins with at least one keyword in SWISS-PROT that matches our trusted vectors (System); cLOCkey: number of proteins for which LOCkey inferred sub-cellular localization in ten classes (Table I; note: these results were obtained using the entropy thresholds that gave 879 testing accuracy; Figure 2); d Homology: sub-cellular localization inferred using homology; i.e. sequence similarity to proteins of known localization taken from SWISS-PROT (at a threshold of HSSP-distance 15; at this distance the assignment through homology yielded levels around 90% accuracy; Nair and Rost; unpublished); € signalP: percentage of predicted extra-cellular proteins also predicted to contain a signal (Nielsen et al., 1997); predictNLS: percentage of predicted nuclear proteins also predicted to have a nuclear localization signal (Cokol et al., 2000). Note that LOCkey enabled to annotate 8352 eukaryotic proteins of unknown localization (19948-11596). bOneKey: peptide","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":290.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.333333333333336,"r":537.5547001962252,"t":360.2968512429633},"charspan":[0,1011],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/26","text":"@Nprot: Number of proteins in proteome; Number of proteins with at least one keyword in SWISS-PROT that matches our trusted vectors (System); cLOCkey: number of proteins for which LOCkey inferred sub-cellular localization in ten classes (Table I; note: these results were obtained using the entropy thresholds that gave 879 testing accuracy; Figure 2); d Homology: sub-cellular localization inferred using homology; i.e. sequence similarity to proteins of known localization taken from SWISS-PROT (at a threshold of HSSP-distance 15; at this distance the assignment through homology yielded levels around 90% accuracy; Nair and Rost; unpublished); € signalP: percentage of predicted extra-cellular proteins also predicted to contain a signal (Nielsen et al., 1997); predictNLS: percentage of predicted nuclear proteins also predicted to have a nuclear localization signal (Cokol et al., 2000). Note that LOCkey enabled to annotate 8352 eukaryotic proteins of unknown localization (19948-11596). bOneKey: peptide"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"the major source of error in predicting nuclear and extra cellular proteins . One reason could be that experimental annotations are less accurate for cytoplasmic   proteins. Another reason could be that proteins do in fact shuttle between the cytoplasm and other localizations  and that our errors' really captured proteins that could also occur in the predicted class. This interpretation was somewhat supported by the finding that LOCkey often found the replacing the   binary   classification accuracy (a   protein can only be in one single localization) by a probabilistic measure (one  protein can be in many   compartments) LOCkey appeared more accurate.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":110.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":36.666666666666664,"r":283.6666666666667,"t":274.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,660],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/27","text":"the major source of error in predicting nuclear and extra cellular proteins . One reason could be that experimental annotations are less accurate for cytoplasmic   proteins. Another reason could be that proteins do in fact shuttle between the cytoplasm and other localizations  and that our errors' really captured proteins that could also occur in the predicted class. This interpretation was somewhat supported by the finding that LOCkey often found the replacing the   binary   classification accuracy (a   protein can only be in one single localization) by a probabilistic measure (one  protein can be in many   compartments) LOCkey appeared more accurate."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"We applied LOCkey to five (yeast; worm, fly; human, and arabidopsis) entirely   sequenced eukaryotic teomes. We could infer localization for over 8300 proteins for which localization could not have been detected by any other automatic system. Three types of methods can infer Or predict localization in the context of entire proteomes: (1) homology to proteins of known localization; (2) detection of sequence motifs; and (3) prediction from sequence and structure. In our group, we simultaneously work on all these types of methods. LOCkey is most relevant for the coverage achieved by homology-based methods; since it allows one to automatically increase the data set of proteins of known localization for which we can apply pro -","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":111.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":296.3333333333333,"r":543.6666666666666,"t":275.0},"charspan":[0,732],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/28","text":"We applied LOCkey to five (yeast; worm, fly; human, and arabidopsis) entirely   sequenced eukaryotic teomes. We could infer localization for over 8300 proteins for which localization could not have been detected by any other automatic system. Three types of methods can infer Or predict localization in the context of entire proteomes: (1) homology to proteins of known localization; (2) detection of sequence motifs; and (3) prediction from sequence and structure. In our group, we simultaneously work on all these types of methods. LOCkey is most relevant for the coverage achieved by homology-based methods; since it allows one to automatically increase the data set of proteins of known localization for which we can apply pro -"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"S84","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":80.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":38.0,"r":56.666666666666664,"t":90.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/29","text":"S84"}],"version":"1.10.0"}